0.059 0.09 0.165 rg
40. 703.8899999999999864 Td
(Q1. What is the mathematical distinction between a symmetric operator and a self-adjoint operator, and why is) Tj
T* (this distinction crucial in quantum mechanics?) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9 Tf
10.3499999999999996 TL
0.2 0.255 0.333 rg
50. 672.8899999999999864 Td
(Ans: A linear operator A on a Hilbert space H is symmetric if < Aphi, psi > = < phi, Apsi > for all phi, psi in the domain D\(A\). It is) Tj
T* (self-adjoint if A = A^*, which explicitly requires that their domains are identical: D\(A\) = D\(A^*\). ) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (This distinction is critical because the Spectral Theorem only applies to self-adjoint operators. Symmetric operators that are not) Tj
T* (self-adjoint may not possess a complete set of orthonormal eigenvectors, and their eigenvalues can be complex or empty. In) Tj
T* (physical terms, only self-adjoint operators guarantee real measurement values \(spectrum sigma\(A\) subset R\) and unitary time) Tj
T* (evolution \(via Stone's theorem on one-parameter unitary groups U\(t\) = e^\(-iHt/hbar\)\).) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.059 0.09 0.165 rg
40. 578.8899999999999864 Td
(Q2. Derive the relation between the density operator's purity and the Von Neumann entropy.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9 Tf
10.3499999999999996 TL
0.2 0.255 0.333 rg
50. 560.8899999999999864 Td
(Ans: Let rho be a density operator. Since rho is positive semidefinite and Tr\(rho\) = 1, it can be diagonalized as rho = sum_i p_i) Tj
T* (|i> < i| where 0 <= p_i <= 1 and \\sum_i p_i = 1\\.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (1. For a **pure state**, exactly one eigenvalue is 1, and all others are 0: p_k = 1, and p_\(i != k\) = 0. ) Tj
T* (   - The purity is Tr\(rho^2\) = sum_i p_i^2 = 1^2 = 1.) Tj
T* (   - The Von Neumann entropy is S\(rho\) = -sum_i p_i ln p_i = -1 ln\(1\) - 0 = 0.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (2. For a **mixed state**, at least two eigenvalues are non-zero, meaning 0 < p_i < 1 for those states.) Tj
T* (   - Since p_i < 1, we have p_i^2 < p_i. Thus, Tr\(rho^2\) = sum_i p_i^2 < sum_i p_i = 1.) Tj
T* (   - Since p_i in \(0, 1\), ln p_i < 0, making the sum S\(rho\) = -sum_i p_i ln p_i > 0.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Thus, a state is pure if and only if purity is 1, which corresponds to an entropy of 0; any mixed state will have purity strictly less) Tj
T* (than 1 and entropy strictly greater than 0.) Tj
